{% from "macros.j2" import q, plot, plain, paras, findings with context %}
{#- Tissue viewer, treatment response and subgroups, biologics and immune. Shares the page's macros and figure dict. -#}
<section class="panel" id="tab-viewer" data-name="viewer">
{{ plain('viewer') }}
<h2>Tissue viewer: what sits next to a gene's high spots? {{ q('tissue_viewer') }}</h2>
<p class="lede">The nine Visium sections of GSE189184 (seven inflamed UC, two healthy colon) are drawn here with their own H&amp;E picture. Pick a gene and the spots in the top tenth of its expression get a ring. Then click any spot: its six neighbours light up and the panel on the right shows what they are made of. The bars compare the neighbours of all high spots with the section average, so a long orange bar means that cell type is over-represented next to the gene.</p>
<div class="howbox"><h4>How to use the viewer</h4><ol>
<li><strong>Pick a section and a gene.</strong> Each dot on the picture is a spot of tissue. The picture underneath is the real stained slice.</li>
<li><strong>Colour by.</strong> Colour the spots by the gene's expression, or by the estimated share of a cell group (epithelium, neutrophil, inflammatory fibroblast and so on). Pale yellow is low and dark red is high.</li>
<li><strong>Orange rings</strong> mark the top tenth of spots for the chosen gene, among spots where it was detected at all.</li>
<li><strong>Click a spot</strong> to outline the six spots around it (about 100 micrometres) and to read its own cell make-up in the box on the right.</li>
<li><strong>The orange and blue bars</strong> compare what surrounds the ringed spots (orange) with the section average (blue). A cell group with a much longer orange bar is over-represented next to the gene.</li></ol></div>
<div class="vctl">
  <label>Section <select id="v-section"></select></label>
  <label>Gene <select id="v-gene"></select></label>
  <label>Colour by <select id="v-color"></select></label>
  <label><input type="checkbox" id="v-rings" checked> ring high spots</label>
  <label>Spot opacity <input type="range" id="v-alpha" min="10" max="100" value="70"></label>
  <label>Spot size <input type="range" id="v-size" min="40" max="160" value="100"></label>
</div>
<div class="vwrap">
  <div><canvas id="vcanvas" width="900" height="900"></canvas><p class="small" id="v-note"></p></div>
  <div>
    <div class="card"><h3 style="margin-top:0">Neighbours of the high spots <span class="small" id="v-nhigh"></span></h3><div id="v-bars"></div>
      <p class="small">Orange: mean share in the six neighbours of the high spots. Blue: the same average over every spot in the section.</p></div>
    <div class="card" style="margin-top:14px"><h3 style="margin-top:0">Selected spot</h3><div id="v-sel"><p class="small">Click a spot on the tissue.</p></div></div>
    <div class="card" style="margin-top:14px"><h3 style="margin-top:0">Tested over the seven UC sections</h3><div id="v-stats"></div>
      <p class="small">Permutation test, adjusted over genes and cell groups. This is the evidence; the live bars above only describe the picked section.</p></div>
  </div>
</div>
<div id="vtip"></div>
</section>

<!-- ================================================================ RESPONSE -->
<section class="panel" id="tab-response" data-name="response">
{{ plain('response') }}
<h2>Which genes predict response to treatment?</h2>
{{ paras(f.narrative.sections.treatment or []) }}
<p class="lede">Biopsies were taken before treatment in four trials and cohorts: UNIFI (ustekinumab and placebo, {{ T.n.ustekinumab }} and {{ T.n.placebo_unifi }} patients with a week-8 endpoint), PURSUIT (golimumab and placebo), ACT1 (infliximab and placebo, {{ T.n.infliximab_act1 }} and {{ T.n.placebo_act1 }} patients, response at week 8), EUCALYPTUS (etrolizumab, {{ T.n.etrolizumab }} patients, remission at week 10), PROTECT (treatment-naive children on corticosteroids, {{ T.n.protect_cs }}, or 5-ASA, {{ T.n.protect_5asa }}; remission at week 4), and the Leuven infliximab and vedolizumab cohorts. For every gene we compare people who later responded (mucosal healing or clinical response, as each study defined it) with people who did not. The question that matters for choosing a drug is different: does the gene work differently with the drug than with placebo? We test that as an interaction between expression and arm.</p>
<div class="note">Before trusting any gene, we checked a published result in this download: the genes reported by Arijs et al. 2009 as higher before treatment in infliximab non-responders ({{ T.check_genes | join(", ") }}) are higher in non-responders of the infliximab cohort, as reported. They are equally higher in placebo non-responders of UNIFI, which suggests that these genes mark how severe the disease is. In ACT1, the trial in which they were found, they are higher in infliximab non-responders and not in the 13 placebo patients; the drug-versus-placebo interaction does not pass correction, so this is a lead for a drug-specific signal, not a result.</div>
<div class="kpis">
  {% for arm, n in T.significant.items() %}<div class="kpi"><b>{{ "{:,}".format(n) }}</b><span>genes separating non-responders, {{ arm }} (q &lt; 0.05)</span></div>{% endfor %}
  <div class="kpi"><b>{{ T.interaction_significant }}</b><span>genes whose effect differs between drug and placebo (q &lt; 0.05)</span></div>
</div>
<div class="howbox"><h4>How to read this section</h4><ul>
<li><strong>The counters</strong> say how many genes differ before treatment between non-responders and responders in each arm, and how many differ between drug and placebo. Many genes in placebo means the signal is disease severity.</li>
<li><strong>Left chart, the check.</strong> Published non-response genes in every arm. Bars above zero: higher in non-responders.</li>
<li><strong>Right chart, every gene.</strong> A dot is a gene. Horizontal: effect in the UNIFI placebo arm. Vertical: effect in the ustekinumab arm. Dots on the diagonal behave the same with drug and placebo.</li>
<li><strong>Tables.</strong> t is non-responders minus responders, q the p-value corrected for about 19,000 genes. A q above 0.05 is a lead and not a result.</li></ul></div>
<div class="grid two">
  {{ plot('treatment_controls', figs.treatment_controls) }}
  {{ plot('treatment_arms', figs.treatment_arms) }}
</div>
{{ plot('response_heatmap', figs.response) }}
<h3>Top genes by arm {{ q('treatment_table') }}</h3>
<div class="grid">
{% for arm, rows in T.top.items() %}
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th colspan="4">{{ arm }}</th></tr><tr><th>Gene</th><th>t (NR − R)</th><th>q</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for r in rows %}<tr><td><strong>{{ r.gene }}</strong></td><td class="num">{{ r.t }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.q }}</td><td>{{ r.direction }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>
{% endfor %}
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th colspan="4">Different between drug and placebo (interaction)</th></tr><tr><th>Gene</th><th>Drug</th><th>log OR interaction</th><th>q</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for r in T.interaction %}<tr><td><strong>{{ r.gene }}</strong></td><td>{{ r.drug }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.lor }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.q }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>
</div>
<p class="small">The interaction is tested only for the candidate genes; none passes correction, so the rows of that table are leads, not findings.</p>

{% if third.antitnf %}{% set At = third.antitnf %}
<h3>Which cells separate remission on anti-TNF? (single cell)</h3>
<p class="small">{{ At.study }}: biopsies of {{ At.uc_patients }} UC patients before and after adalimumab ({{ At.uc_remission }} remission, {{ At.uc_non_remission }} non-remission), about one million cells in 109 cell states annotated by the authors; the authors' published per-cell-state models are used (the count matrices in GEO carry no cell annotation). Before treatment, the cell states whose abundance differs: {% for x in At.abundance_cell_states_padj05 %}{{ x.cell_state | replace("_", " ") }} (OR {{ x.or_nonremission }}){{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}. In the table, a positive baseline value means higher in non-remission; a positive after-treatment value means higher after adalimumab.</p>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Gene</th><th>Before treatment, non-remission vs remission (cell state, coef)</th><th>After vs before treatment (cell state, coef)</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for r in third.antitnf_rows %}<tr{% if r.evidence_ids %} class="claim" data-evidence="{{ r.evidence_ids | evattr }}"{% endif %}><td><strong>{{ r.gene }}</strong></td><td>{{ r.baseline }}</td><td>{{ r.after }}</td><td>{{ r.evidence_ids | cite | safe }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="small">Six remitters against 14 non-remitters: leads, not predictors. DUOX2 and LCN2 are lower in colonocytes of non-remitters before treatment, so this response signal is in the epithelium, not in the infiltrating cells that dominate the bulk biopsies.</p>{% endif %}
{% if third.calprotectin %}<h3>Does the tissue signal follow faecal calprotectin?</h3>
<p class="small">Faecal calprotectin (the S100A8/A9 protein in stool) is the non-invasive marker used in clinics. Across {{ third.calprotectin_n }} PROTECT children, Spearman correlation of each gene in the rectal biopsy with baseline calprotectin: {% for r in third.calprotectin %}{{ r.gene }} {{ r.rho }} (padj {{ r.padj }}){{ "; " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}. Correlations are weak: stool calprotectin reflects the whole colon, the biopsy one site.</p>{% endif %}
<h2>Does the answer change with the patient?</h2>
{{ paras(f.narrative.sections.subgroups or []) }}
{% set S2 = layers.subgroups %}
<p class="lede">A target that matters for children with extensive colitis may not matter for adults with proctitis. Three splits are available in the public cohorts: <strong>age</strong> (the paediatric RNA-seq cohorts PROTECT and RISK against five adult cohorts), <strong>biopsy site</strong> (in MSCCR, inflamed against control biopsies from the rectum, {{ S2.site_counts.n_rectum[0] }} against {{ S2.site_counts.n_rectum[1] }} patients, and from the proximal colon, {{ S2.site_counts.n_proximal[0] }} against {{ S2.site_counts.n_proximal[1] }}) and <strong>activity</strong> (the endoscopic Mayo subscore).</p>
<div class="howbox"><h4>How to read this section</h4><ul>
<li><strong>Left chart.</strong> How many genes rise in each cohort and at each MSCCR site. Paediatric cohorts (orange) are treatment-naive and very inflamed, which is why they show the most genes.</li>
<li><strong>Right chart.</strong> The activity axis: each biopsy's neutrophil score (marker genes of infiltrating neutrophils) by endoscopic Mayo grade. It is the UC counterpart of a tumour subtype score: a continuous axis inside the disease.</li>
<li><strong>Tables.</strong> Genes that rise in one subgroup and not in the other. They are candidates for one kind of patient, not for every patient.</li></ul></div>
<div class="grid two">
  {{ plot('subgroup_counts', figs.subgroup_counts) }}
  {{ plot('activity_axis', figs.activity_axis) }}
</div>
<p class="small">Age: {{ S2.age_counts.paediatric_only }} genes rise in both paediatric cohorts but in at most one adult cohort; {{ S2.age_counts.adult_only }} rise in at least four adult cohorts and in neither paediatric one. Site: {{ S2.site_counts.rectum }} genes rise in the rectum and {{ S2.site_counts.proximal }} in the proximal colon; the fold changes of the two sites correlate at rho {{ S2.site_counts.rho_log2fc }}, so most of the response is shared and the site-specific lists are short. Activity: the neutrophil score rises with the Mayo grade (rho {{ S2.activity_axis.spearman_rho }}, n = {{ S2.activity_axis.n }} UC biopsies).</p>
<h3>Subgroup candidates {{ q('subgroup_table') }}</h3>
<div class="grid">
{% for name, rows in S2.tables.items() %}{% set cols = S2.table_columns[name] %}
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th colspan="6">{{ name }}</th></tr>
<tr><th>Gene</th><th>{{ cols[0] }}</th><th>{{ cols[1] }}</th><th>{{ cols[2] }}</th><th>Tractable</th><th>Clinical drug</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for r in rows %}<tr><td><strong>{{ r.gene }}</strong></td><td class="num">{{ r.a }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.b }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.c }}</td><td>{{ "yes" if r.predicted_tractable else "no" }}</td><td>{{ "yes" if r.clinical_drug else "no" }}</td></tr>
{% else %}<tr><td colspan="6" class="small">No gene passes the tests.</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>
{% endfor %}
</div>
<p class="small">Paediatric and adult cohorts also differ in platform (all paediatric cohorts are RNA-seq) and in how inflamed the biopsies are, so a gene in the paediatric list may reflect severity rather than age.</p>
{{ findings('applied', 'Findings from the translational agent') }}
</section>

<!-- ================================================================ BIOLOGICS AND IMMUNE -->
<section class="panel" id="tab-biologics" data-name="biologics">
{{ plain('biologics') }}
{% set B = layers.biologics %}
<h2>Antibody and biologic targets {{ q('biologics_table') }}</h2>
{{ paras(f.narrative.sections.biologics or []) }}
<p class="lede">An antibody needs more than a gene that goes up. The protein has to sit on the cell surface or be secreted, it should be low in vital organs, and most patients have to carry it. Each of {{ B.candidates }} genes up in UC gets four scores: surface or secreted location (Open Targets antibody buckets from UniProt and GO), increase in inflamed mucosa, low RNA in vital organs (Human Protein Atlas) and the share of inflamed MSCCR patients above the 95th percentile of controls. {{ B.surface }} have a surface or secreted annotation. Antibody targets of UC drugs are controls and rank {% for g, c in B.controls.items() %}{{ g }} {{ c.rank }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}: most of them do not rise in biopsies, so this score finds new handles in the inflamed tissue rather than the known ones.</p>
<div class="howbox"><h4>How the suitability score is built and read</h4><ul>
<li><strong>Four parts, each from 0 to 1.</strong> Surface ({{ B.weights.surface }}): 1 for a high-confidence UniProt surface or secreted location, 0.7 for GO or medium confidence, 0.5 for a signal peptide or transmembrane segment. Increase ({{ B.weights.increase }}): meta log2FC up to 3. Vital organs ({{ B.weights.vital_low }}): 1 minus the highest vital-organ nTPM divided by 100. Patients ({{ B.weights.carriers }}): share of inflamed patients above the control 95th percentile, where 50 % counts as full marks.</li>
<li><strong>The chart.</strong> Horizontal: increase. Vertical: patients carrying it. Colour: vital-organ RNA, pale is low. Size: surface or secreted. The best dots are far right, high up, pale and large.</li>
<li><strong>Controls.</strong> Known UC antibody targets are diamonds; most sit low because their mRNA barely changes in biopsies.</li></ul></div>
<div class="grid two">
  {{ plot('biologics', figs.biologics) }}
  <div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Gene</th><th>Score</th><th>Meta log2FC</th><th>Surface</th><th>Vital-organ nTPM</th><th>Patients carrying (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  {% for r in bio_top %}<tr><td>{{ r.rank }}</td><td><strong>{{ r.gene }}</strong>{% if r.control %} <span class="tag">control</span>{% endif %}</td><td class="num">{{ r.suitability }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.meta_log2fc | fmt }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.c_surface }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.vital_max_ntpm | fmt }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.patients_carrying_pct | fmt }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </tbody></table></div>
</div>
<p class="small">Vital organs: heart muscle, brain regions, liver, lung and kidney (HPA consensus RNA). Weights fixed before ranking. RNA in normal tissue is not protein staining.</p>

{% if third.safety %}{% set Sf = third.safety %}
<h2>Safety of the leading targets</h2>
<p class="lede">Three independent views. Loss-of-function constraint (gnomAD v4.1 LOEUF; below 0.35 people rarely carry a broken copy, so life-long inhibition may be poorly tolerated): {{ Sf.constrained_candidates }} candidates and {{ Sf.constrained_clinical_targets }} of {{ Sf.clinical_targets }} UC clinical targets are strongly constrained (median LOEUF {{ Sf.median_loeuf_candidates }} vs {{ Sf.median_loeuf_clinical_targets }}). Known safety events (Open Targets): {{ Sf.candidates_with_safety_events }} candidates, {{ Sf.clinical_targets_with_safety_events }} clinical targets. Other conditions sharing the gene's genetic signal (PheWAS of the MR instruments): {{ Sf.genes_with_phewas }} genes, for example psoriasis for IL12B, the other indication of ustekinumab.</p>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Gene</th><th class="num">LOEUF</th><th>Strongly constrained</th><th>Known safety events</th><th>Other conditions sharing the genetic signal</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for r in third.safety_rows %}<tr{% if r.evidence_ids %} class="claim" data-evidence="{{ r.evidence_ids | evattr }}"{% endif %}><td><strong>{{ r.gene }}</strong></td><td class="num">{{ r.loeuf | fmt }}</td><td>{{ "yes" if r.constrained else "no" }}</td><td>{{ r.events }}</td><td>{{ r.phewas }}</td><td>{{ r.evidence_ids | cite | safe }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="small">Constraint and pleiotropy flag possible on-target risks; many approved drugs hit constrained genes. Safety events are curated reports for the target in any indication.</p>{% endif %}

{% set I = layers.immune %}
<h2>Immune microenvironment {{ q('immune_table') }}</h2>
{{ paras(f.narrative.sections.immune or []) }}
<p class="lede">Here immune genes are the target rather than infiltration noise. Each of {{ I.patients }} MSCCR patients (inflamed UC and control biopsies) gets an immune score from the marker genes of T/NK cells, myeloid cells, neutrophils, B cells and plasma cells, and the patients are split into thirds: {{ I.classes.low }} low, {{ I.classes.intermediate }} intermediate and {{ I.classes.high }} high ({{ "%.0f" | format(100 * I.inflamed_share_by_class.high) }} % of the high third are inflamed UC biopsies). We then ask which genes follow T/NK, myeloid and neutrophil infiltration across patients, combine that with the single-cell source, the increase in UC, tractability and the ustekinumab response, and rank every gene. Seven immune targets of UC drugs serve as controls; {{ I.controls_in_top100 }} of them reach the top 100 (ranks {% for g, r in I.controls.items() %}{{ g }} {{ r }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %} of {{ "{:,}".format(I.universe) }}). So this profile finds the neutrophil and myeloid biology of inflamed mucosa but does not rediscover the known targets, which act on cytokines and lymphocyte traffic whose mRNA barely changes; read it as a way to list candidates, not as a validated ranking.</p>
<div class="howbox"><h4>How to read this section</h4><ul>
<li><strong>Low, intermediate, high.</strong> Patients are split into thirds by immune content. The chart shows each lineage score in each third, rising from low to high.</li>
<li><strong>The table</strong> ranks genes for immune-target potential by adding five parts: immune share of the gene's transcripts ({{ I.weights.immune_share }}), coupling with infiltration ({{ I.weights.coupling }}), increase in UC ({{ I.weights.up }}), tractability ({{ I.weights.tractable }}) and higher expression in ustekinumab non-responders ({{ I.weights.response }}).</li></ul></div>
<div class="grid two">
  {{ plot('immune_classes', figs.immune_classes) }}
  <div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Gene</th><th>Score</th><th>Immune share</th><th>ρ T/NK</th><th>ρ myeloid</th><th>ρ neutrophil</th><th>Meta log2FC</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  {% for r in imm_top %}<tr><td>{{ r.rank }}</td><td><strong>{{ r.gene }}</strong></td><td class="num">{{ r.immune_target_score }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.c_immune_share }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.rho_T_NK }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.rho_myeloid }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.rho_neutrophil }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.meta_log2fc | fmt }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </tbody></table></div>
</div>
</section>

<script id="viewer-data" type="application/gzip-base64" data-asset="viewer.b64">{{ viewer_b64 }}</script>
<script>
(function(){
  var V=null, loading=null, $=function(id){return document.getElementById(id);};
  var cv, ctx, tip, cur=null, img=null, sel=-1, highIdx=[], secSel, geneSel, colSel;
  var NAMES={Epithelial:'Epithelium',Fibroblast:'Fibroblast',Inflammatory_fibroblast:'Inflammatory fibroblast',Smooth_muscle:'Smooth muscle',Endothelial:'Endothelium',Myeloid:'Macrophage / DC',Neutrophil:'Neutrophil',T_NK:'T / NK',B:'B cell',Plasma:'Plasma',Mast:'Mast',Eosinophil:'Eosinophil'};
  function ramp(t){t=Math.max(0,Math.min(1,t));var a=[255,237,160],b=[177,0,38];return [a[0]+(b[0]-a[0])*t,a[1]+(b[1]-a[1])*t,a[2]+(b[2]-a[2])*t];}
  function values(){var s=cur;if(colSel.value==='gene'){var e=s.expr[geneSel.value]||[];return {v:e,max:Math.max.apply(null,e.concat([1]))};}
    var sh=s.shares[colSel.value];return {v:sh,max:Math.max.apply(null,sh.concat([1]))};}
  function computeHigh(){var e=cur.expr[geneSel.value]||[];var pos=[];e.forEach(function(x){if(x>0)pos.push(x);});
    highIdx=[];if(pos.length<20)return; pos.sort(function(a,b){return a-b;});var thr=pos[Math.floor(pos.length*0.9)];
    e.forEach(function(x,i){if(x>0&&x>=thr)highIdx.push(i);});}
  function nbMean(i){var nb=cur.neighbors[i];if(!nb.length)return null;var out={};V.groups.forEach(function(g){var t=0;nb.forEach(function(j){t+=cur.shares[g][j];});out[g]=t/nb.length;});return out;}
  function bars(){var box=$('v-bars');box.innerHTML='';computeHigh();$('v-nhigh').textContent=highIdx.length?'('+highIdx.length+' high spots)':'';
    if(!highIdx.length){box.innerHTML='<p class="small">Too few spots with this gene in this section (fewer than 20). Pick another section or gene.</p>';return;}
    var obs={},exp={},nh=0,ne=0;V.groups.forEach(function(g){obs[g]=0;exp[g]=0;});
    highIdx.forEach(function(i){var m=nbMean(i);if(m){nh++;V.groups.forEach(function(g){obs[g]+=m[g];});}});
    for(var i=0;i<cur.n_spots;i++){var m2=nbMean(i);if(m2){ne++;V.groups.forEach(function(g){exp[g]+=m2[g];});}}
    var rows=V.groups.map(function(g){return {g:g,o:obs[g]/Math.max(nh,1),e:exp[g]/Math.max(ne,1)};});
    rows.sort(function(a,b){return (b.o/Math.max(b.e,0.01))-(a.o/Math.max(a.e,0.01));});
    var mx=Math.max.apply(null,rows.map(function(r){return Math.max(r.o,r.e);}).concat([1]));
    rows.forEach(function(r){var d=document.createElement('div');d.innerHTML='<div class="bar"><span class="lab">'+(NAMES[r.g]||r.g)+'</span><span class="track"><span class="fill b" style="display:block;width:'+(100*r.o/mx)+'%"></span></span><span class="val">'+r.o.toFixed(1)+' %</span></div><div class="bar"><span class="lab"></span><span class="track"><span class="fill" style="display:block;width:'+(100*r.e/mx)+'%"></span></span><span class="val">'+r.e.toFixed(1)+' %</span></div>';box.appendChild(d);});}
  function stats(){var box=$('v-stats'),g=geneSel.value,st=V.stats[g],n=V.stats_n[g];
    if(!st){box.innerHTML='<p class="small">No permutation result for this gene.</p>';return;}
    var rows=st.slice().sort(function(a,b){return b.log2-a.log2;}).filter(function(r){return r.padj<0.05;});
    var h='<p class="small">'+n.n_high_spots+' high spots in '+n.n_sections+' UC sections.</p>';
    if(!rows.length)h+='<p class="small">No cell group is significantly enriched or depleted next to this gene.</p>';
    else{h+='<table><thead><tr><th>Cell group</th><th>log2 obs/exp</th><th>q</th></tr></thead><tbody>';rows.forEach(function(r){h+='<tr><td>'+(NAMES[r.cell_group]||r.cell_group)+'</td><td class="num">'+(r.log2>0?'+':'')+r.log2.toFixed(2)+'</td><td class="num">'+r.padj+'</td></tr>';});h+='</tbody></table>';}
    box.innerHTML=h;}
  function draw(){if(!img||!cur)return;var W=cv.parentNode.clientWidth||900;cv.width=W;cv.height=Math.round(W*cur.height/cur.width);var k=W/cur.width;
    ctx.clearRect(0,0,cv.width,cv.height);ctx.drawImage(img,0,0,cv.width,cv.height);
    var vals=values(),alpha=$('v-alpha').value/100,rad=cur.spot_px*k/2*($('v-size').value/100);
    for(var i=0;i<cur.n_spots;i++){var c=ramp(vals.v[i]/vals.max);var a=vals.v[i]>0?alpha*(0.25+0.75*vals.v[i]/vals.max):alpha*0.08;
      ctx.fillStyle='rgba('+c[0]+','+c[1]+','+c[2]+','+a+')';ctx.beginPath();ctx.arc(cur.x[i]*k,cur.y[i]*k,rad,0,6.2832);ctx.fill();}
    if($('v-rings').checked){ctx.strokeStyle='#eb6834';ctx.lineWidth=1.6;highIdx.forEach(function(i){ctx.beginPath();ctx.arc(cur.x[i]*k,cur.y[i]*k,rad+1.5,0,6.2832);ctx.stroke();});}
    if(sel>=0){ctx.strokeStyle='#1c1c1b';ctx.lineWidth=1.4;cur.neighbors[sel].forEach(function(j){ctx.beginPath();ctx.arc(cur.x[j]*k,cur.y[j]*k,rad+2.5,0,6.2832);ctx.stroke();});
      ctx.lineWidth=2.6;ctx.strokeStyle='#e34948';ctx.beginPath();ctx.arc(cur.x[sel]*k,cur.y[sel]*k,rad+3,0,6.2832);ctx.stroke();}}
  function nearest(ev){var r=cv.getBoundingClientRect(),k=cv.width/cur.width,mx=(ev.clientX-r.left)*(cv.width/r.width)/k,my=(ev.clientY-r.top)*(cv.height/r.height)/k,best=-1,bd=1e9,lim=cur.spot_px*1.2;
    for(var i=0;i<cur.n_spots;i++){var dx=cur.x[i]-mx,dy=cur.y[i]-my,d=dx*dx+dy*dy;if(d<bd){bd=d;best=i;}}return bd<lim*lim?best:-1;}
  function showSel(){var box=$('v-sel');if(sel<0){box.innerHTML='<p class="small">Click a spot on the tissue.</p>';return;}
    var m=nbMean(sel),g=geneSel.value,e=(cur.expr[g]||[])[sel]||0;var h='<p class="small">'+g+' in this spot: log1p CPM '+(e/V.expr_scale).toFixed(2)+(highIdx.indexOf(sel)>=0?' (a high spot)':'')+'. Six neighbours are outlined.</p>';
    var rows=V.groups.map(function(x){return {g:x,s:cur.shares[x][sel],n:m?m[x]:0};}).sort(function(a,b){return b.s-a.s;}).slice(0,6);
    rows.forEach(function(r){h+='<div class="bar"><span class="lab">'+(NAMES[r.g]||r.g)+'</span><span class="track"><span class="fill" style="display:block;width:'+r.s+'%"></span></span><span class="val">'+r.s+' % · nb '+r.n.toFixed(0)+' %</span></div>';});
    box.innerHTML=h;}
  function load(){cur=V.sections[+secSel.value];sel=-1;img=new Image();img.onload=function(){refresh();};img.src=cur.image;}
  function refresh(){computeHigh();bars();stats();showSel();draw();$('v-note').textContent=(cur.region==='healthy'?'Healthy colon section. ':'Inflamed UC section. ')+'Left to right and top to bottom are image directions, not anatomy.';}
  function setup(){
    cv=$('vcanvas'); ctx=cv.getContext('2d'); tip=$('vtip'); secSel=$('v-section'); geneSel=$('v-gene'); colSel=$('v-color');
    V.sections.forEach(function(s,i){var o=document.createElement('option');o.value=i;o.textContent=s.region+' · '+s.donor+' · '+s.n_spots+' spots';secSel.appendChild(o);});
    V.genes.forEach(function(g){var o=document.createElement('option');o.value=g;o.textContent=g;geneSel.appendChild(o);});
    geneSel.value=V.genes.indexOf('S100A8')>=0?'S100A8':V.genes[0];
    var co=document.createElement('option');co.value='gene';co.textContent='Gene expression';colSel.appendChild(co);
    V.groups.forEach(function(g){var o=document.createElement('option');o.value=g;o.textContent=(NAMES[g]||g)+' share';colSel.appendChild(o);});
    secSel.addEventListener('change',load);geneSel.addEventListener('change',function(){sel=-1;refresh();});colSel.addEventListener('change',draw);
    ['v-rings','v-alpha','v-size'].forEach(function(id){$(id).addEventListener('input',draw);});
    cv.addEventListener('click',function(ev){sel=nearest(ev);showSel();draw();});
    cv.addEventListener('mousemove',function(ev){var i=nearest(ev);if(i<0){tip.style.display='none';return;}var g=geneSel.value,e=((cur.expr[g]||[])[i]||0)/V.expr_scale;
      var top=V.groups.slice().sort(function(a,b){return cur.shares[b][i]-cur.shares[a][i];})[0];tip.style.display='block';tip.style.left=(ev.clientX+12)+'px';tip.style.top=(ev.clientY+12)+'px';
      tip.textContent=g+' '+e.toFixed(2)+' · mostly '+(NAMES[top]||top)+' ('+cur.shares[top][i]+' %)';});
    cv.addEventListener('mouseleave',function(){tip.style.display='none';});
    window.addEventListener('resize',draw);
  }
  // the data (H&E pictures and spots) is inflated, or fetched on the published site, the first time the tab opens
  function ready(){if(!loading){$('v-note').textContent='Loading the tissue sections...';
    loading=window.atlasPayload('viewer-data').then(function(x){V=x; if(V&&V.sections&&V.sections.length) setup(); return V;});}
    return loading;}
  window.viewerShow=function(){ready().then(function(){if(!V||!V.sections||!V.sections.length)return; if(!cur)load(); else draw();});};
})();
</script>
